marc | wolovim: Yeah, it looks good.

85
00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:22.950
Anders Elowsson: Okay, great. Yeah, so essentially, we want to discuss, you know, the interaction between Frame transactions and a multidimensional fee-market.

86
00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:26.510
Anders Elowsson: Oh, let's see.

87
00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:32.559
Anders Elowsson: Because Frame transactions aim to be this enduring transaction format.

88
00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:45.190
Anders Elowsson: And, we want to do a multidimensional fee-market in the future, and we want to then sync, you know, the research and fee fields so that we have a seamless transition.

89
00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:52.729
Anders Elowsson: And there's also the Frame transactions change how gas is accounted for inside the EVM.

90
00:17:52.950 --> 00:18:00.499
Anders Elowsson: And this is something that the multidimensional fee-market also needs to do, and so we should try to make sure that these two fit together.

91
00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:21.100
Anders Elowsson: And so there are three things I want to discuss. One idea is to have byte limits for state instead of gas limits for the user. The other is to have a unified max fee instead of a separate max fees per resource, and then to have this discussion about how the EVM gas accounting should be designed.

92
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:28.540
Anders Elowsson: So today, with EIP 8037, we are adding, its own metering to state.

93
00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:35.649
Anders Elowsson: And if we were to go to a multidimensional fee-market, we would turn all of these nos into a yes.

94
00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:40.590
Anders Elowsson: So it would look something like this. There will be 1 meter per resource.

95
00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:54.210
Anders Elowsson: One base fee per resource, and this helps scaling, because developers can set a safe limit per resource. The market can price each one, and every resource can be used up to its own limit.

96
00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:03.780
Anders Elowsson: And one thing that EIP 7999 does is that it says one single max feed that is fungible across resources.

97
00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:19.499
Anders Elowsson: A reason why we would like to do that is that base fees, they can vary independently, and so even though you set it correctly at… when you send a transaction, it would be enough that one base fee of one of the resources would exceed the maximum.

98
00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:26.220
Anders Elowsson: For the transaction to fail, even though the user is willing to pay enough for the transaction to proceed.

99
00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:43.019
Anders Elowsson: And when you set one single max fee, you can allow the transaction to be executed, even because you don't have these separate limits that can preclude inclusion, even though the user is willing to pay.

100
00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:52.769
Anders Elowsson: There's also some, of course, some minor UX and, for example, dev benefits of having one number here for the max fee instead of several.

101
00:19:53.750 --> 00:20:01.879
Anders Elowsson: So, for Frame Transactions, you would turn the max fee per gas and max fee per blob gas into a single max fee at first.

102
00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:06.689
Anders Elowsson: And then, if we add more resources, we would keep the same max fee.

103
00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:14.630
Anders Elowsson: The priority, however, should still be probably per gas, so that you can link tips to actual consumption.

104
00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.610
Anders Elowsson: And the second idea is to do,

105
00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:29.219
Anders Elowsson: a state limit in bytes instead of doing it in gas. And this makes it such that we can be safe across repricing.

106
00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:30.400
Anders Elowsson: So…

107
00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:44.769
Anders Elowsson: We can protect users across fork boundaries when the cost per state bike is adjusted. If the limit is denominated in gas, and the cost per state bike rises, then the transaction may suddenly fail.

108
00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.220
Anders Elowsson: So, for example.

109
00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:56.139
Anders Elowsson: Besides protecting users, it also means that wallets do not need to look up the cost per state price to express the limit.

110
00:20:57.340 --> 00:21:00.949
Anders Elowsson: It also, of course, is a more compact encoding.

111
00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:04.869
Anders Elowsson: And it is the natural unit of account.

112
00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:15.899
Anders Elowsson: So these are some reasons for using bytes instead of gas for the state limit, and any future limits in natural units.

113
00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:17.950
Anders Elowsson: And,

114
00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:31.870
Anders Elowsson: This can stay very simple. You can use, you can convert such a state-back limits expressed in the transaction to a gas limit immediately upon processing the transaction.

115
00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:37.940
Anders Elowsson: So you could conserve the old processing scheme that you have.

116
00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:45.370
Anders Elowsson: And then finally, the final point here is to… to,

117
00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:56.040
Anders Elowsson: to discuss how to do EVM gas accounting, and we have been trying to sort this out for the multidimensional fee-market, because you price resources separately.

118
00:21:56.240 --> 00:22:11.359
Anders Elowsson: While today the EVM works with a single limit, and we want to preserve legacy call operations and various operations, we then have to make various trade-offs, and there are then different designs that have been under consideration.

119
00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:17.670
Anders Elowsson: One is to have an aggregate EVM gas, so you have an aggregate gas limit for all resources.

120
00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:25.990
Anders Elowsson: But since they are separate fees, that means that any resource that can consume the gas limit, so it must be funded at the highest base fee.

121
00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:32.399
Anders Elowsson: Then, there's another design that, called Sub-fee Accounting.

122
00:22:32.600 --> 00:22:36.050
Anders Elowsson: And here you have, instead, floating gas prices.

123
00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:42.740
Anders Elowsson: And so the user science limits in native units, which are converted at the relative baseway of the block.

124
00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:45.290
Anders Elowsson: To an aggregate gas limit.

125
00:22:45.650 --> 00:23:02.489
Anders Elowsson: And this means that you parry, you don't avoid… you avoid this problem with the funding shake, but then you have these floating gas prices, so the developer needs to query the gas price if it wants to preserve in a sort of a gas way.

126
00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:04.350
Anders Elowsson: Of the design.

127
00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:10.850
Anders Elowsson: And then, the full multidimensional fee-market, where you preserve these separate limits.

128
00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:12.840
Anders Elowsson: for each resource.

129
00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:15.480
Anders Elowsson: And…

130
00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:27.429
Anders Elowsson: The problem then is, if we have this full multidimensional fee-market, we have to consider how to treat all these legacy gas operations, for example, legacy coals, etc.

131
00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:41.290
Anders Elowsson: And of course, you could just send in proportional for each resource based on the limits, but it's not really perfect for what you would like to do, probably.

132
00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:49.739
Anders Elowsson: And so, the idea then is, one idea is to have a universal overflow channel instead.

133
00:23:50.640 --> 00:24:00.070
Anders Elowsson: And so that would be a separate resource in addition to the other resource… resources that you have.

134
00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:15.960
Anders Elowsson: And when you do a call, for example, then the gas perimeter relates to the universal overflow, so you can control how much you preserve after the universal overflow, and how much is sent into the call.

135
00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:21.079
Anders Elowsson: Whereas all the other resources are sent in full.

136
00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:33.879
Anders Elowsson: And if a resource consumes fully its own limit, its own budget, then it starts drawing from the universal overflow.

137
00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:50.219
Anders Elowsson: And after processing the subcall, you then have the universal overflow that was reserved back in it, and can be used then for any future operations within one frame, then, if we're talking about the Frame transactions.

138
00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:58.779
Anders Elowsson: And this universal overflow could be funded according to any of the three different,

139
00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:09.250
Anders Elowsson: the signs that I mentioned in the previous slides, so that means that we have 6 different options that have been under consideration for the gas accounting.

140
00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:19.609
Anders Elowsson: And if we then look at frames, the new thing of the design here is concerning the state gas, which is a frame-level pool.

141
00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:25.130
Anders Elowsson: When nested calls, it draws from it directly, and it's not forwarded or split.

142
00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:31.650
Anders Elowsson: And so, this has some implications, then, for these legacy gas operations.

143
00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:44.640
Anders Elowsson: Both for execution gas and for state gas. So, for example, can a caller reserve capacity for work after a subcall? For execution gas, yes. For state gas, between frames only.

144
00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:52.289
Anders Elowsson: Can a caller cap the work that the caller performed? For execution goes, yes, and for state guts between frames only.

145
00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:59.410
Anders Elowsson: And DAS measure all remaining capacity? It measures execution gas only, and it does not measure state.

146
00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:11.360
Anders Elowsson: And looking at these two different designs, then, there are a few questions that come up that would be interesting to study or discuss further, I guess.

147
00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:12.200
Anders Elowsson: Yeah.

148
00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:23.989
Anders Elowsson: And one is, how much of the reservation problem can separate transaction frames solve? How much are we solving in frames now? And which needs to remain within ordinary calls?

149
00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:31.850
Anders Elowsson: And what are the implications of this frame… frame design on the need for a universal overflow?

150
00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:41.890
Anders Elowsson: And then, holistically, what should a design identity to both frames and multi-dimensional fee-market look like when it comes to the EVM gas accounting?

151
00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:46.309
Anders Elowsson: So that makes it, 3 different,

152
00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:55.420
Anders Elowsson: ideas here that we can discuss further, the byte-limited idea, the unified max fee idea, and how to proceed with the EVM gas accounting.

153
